{"type": "text", "timestamp": 1793897538522, "sessionID": "ses_e53602ac3ffeZtqhU7KKUDxuAn", "part": {"id": "prt_1aca01551001MZf7mbXdaBm424", "messageID": "msg_1aca00cca0017hF130JzNCqBla", "sessionID": "ses_e53602ac3ffeZtqhU7KKUDxuAn", "type": "text", "text": "**OrderService** and **RenewalsWorker** call **PaymentsAPI** directly, according to the architecture graph:\n- `OrderService \u2192 CALLS \u2192 PaymentsAPI`\n- `RenewalsWorker \u2192 CALLS \u2192 PaymentsAPI`\n\n**The available tools do not establish why PaymentsService never retries a declined payment.** They expose dependencies and configuration relationships, but no retry policy, decline-handling logic, or rationale.\n\nFor **PaymentsService**, the tools identify:\n- Configuration keys: `PAYMENT_API_KEY` and `CAPTURE_TIMEOUT`. Neither result describes retry behavior.\n- Outbound API: **NotificationsAPI**, exposed by **NotificationsService**.\n- Database: **PaymentsPostgresDatabase**.\n- Indirect storage dependency: **NotificationsRedisInstance**, used by **NotificationsService**.\n\nThe no-retry rationale would need to be confirmed from PaymentsService\u2019s implementation or payment-policy documentation. Attributing it to either configuration key would be speculation.", "time": {"start": 1793897534802, "end": 1793897538510}, "metadata": {"openai": {"itemId": "msg_04e89e11cdb7b897016aecb43eb01087d1a2ea7a0dabec4e65", "phase": "final_answer"}}}}
